  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "properties indexers fields methods parameters and overloads",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Fields store type state directly; properties expose controlled read/write behavior; methods represent actions; and indexers provide array-like access to an object. Choose the member form that expresses the contract rather than selecting syntax by habit.",
    "An accessor property may validate, compute, or delegate access. Auto-properties synthesize backing storage; `init` and `required` express different construction rules, and nullable annotations do not replace runtime validation.",
    "Methods are selected through overload resolution from the compile-time arguments, conversions, generic inference, and candidate signatures. Optional and named arguments affect calls but do not create different runtime method identities.",
    "Parameter modifiers define how values cross a call boundary. `ref`, `in`, `out`, `ref readonly`, `params`, optional parameters, and `scoped` each impose distinct caller and implementation rules.",
    "Indexers and extension members let APIs use natural access syntax, but they should remain discoverable and unsurprising. C# 14 extension blocks add extension properties and static members; extension methods remain supported.",
    "Member modifiers interact with inheritance and dispatch. Static members belong to a type; virtual/override members participate in runtime dispatch; `new` hides; `partial` lets generated code contribute a declaration."
  ],
